A 15-year-old can qualify under the policy's adolescent criteria for patients aged 12 to 17. All of the following must be met:

1. **Weight category (one of):**
   - Class III obesity; or
   - Class II obesity with at least one of these comorbidities:
     - Insulin resistance or type 2 diabetes;
     - Poorly controlled hypertension (systolic over 140 mm Hg or diastolic 90 mm Hg or higher, despite pharmacotherapy);
     - Hyperlipidemia;
     - Obstructive sleep apnea confirmed on polysomnography, with an Apnea-Hypopnea Index or Respiratory Disturbance Index of 30 or higher;
     - Evidence of Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease; or
     - Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri). [uhc-bariatric-surgery.pdf p.2]

2. **Evaluation at a specialized center:** The individual must be evaluated at, or in consultation with, a multidisciplinary center focused on surgical treatment of severe childhood obesity. This may include adolescent centers accredited by the Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery Accreditation and Quality Improvement Program, or centers that can demonstrate similar programmatic components. [uhc-bariatric-surgery.pdf p.2]

The policy also lists a general requirement that the individual must complete both a preoperative evaluation (a detailed weight history plus dietary and physical activity patterns) and a psychosocial-behavioral evaluation by a behavioral health professional, or participate in a multidisciplinary surgical preparatory regimen. This requirement appears in the policy text just before the adolescent criteria, and the wording does not clearly say whether it applies to adolescents as well as adults. [uhc-bariatric-surgery.pdf p.2]

The policy covers gastric bypass, sleeve gastrectomy, and biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch for these patients. Adjustable gastric banding is limited to individuals 18 and older. [uhc-bariatric-surgery.pdf p.1]
